      <p class="toy-caption">A real Tokio scheduler is work-stealing and time-based, not this discrete queue. The toy isolates the key behavior: <code>await</code> frees a worker during host I/O; a fuel/epoch yield bounds how long CPU-heavy guest code stays inside one future poll.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="wasmtime-async" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.3 · runtime mechanics</span>
        <h2>What happens on a Tokio worker thread.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="poll-cycle">
        <div><span>Tokio worker thread</span><strong>polls Wasmtime call future</strong><small>guest executes on this OS thread</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div><span>Wasmtime fiber</span><strong>runs guest stack</strong><small>separate native stack, not a separate OS thread</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div><span>one of two suspension points</span><strong>host await or compute yield</strong><small>future returns <code>Pending</code></small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div><span>Tokio runtime</span><strong>polls another ready task</strong><small>original task resumes later</small></div>
      </div>

      <h3>Suspension path A: an asynchronous host import</h3>
      <p>A future privileged provider calls a narrow WIT import. The linker’s Rust implementation starts an asynchronous request. If that future is not ready, Wasmtime switches off the guest’s fiber stack and propagates <code>Pending</code>. From the guest’s synchronous point of view the import is still waiting; from the host’s point of view the OS thread is free.</p>

      <h3>Suspension path B: guest compute reaches a yield check</h3>
      <p>Pure Wasm does not naturally <code>.await</code> Tokio. Without instrumentation it may run inside one <code>poll</code> for an arbitrarily long time. Wasmtime can make an async Store yield after an interval of consumed fuel, or when an engine epoch reaches the Store deadline and the Store is configured to yield and update.</p>

      <div class="yield-comparison">
        <article><div><span>fuel</span>{{ status("exploration", "Candidate") }}</div><h3>Deterministic work slices</h3><p><code>consume_fuel(true)</code> instruments execution. <code>fuel_async_yield_interval(Some(n))</code> yields every <em>n</em> units while the larger Store fuel budget still traps at zero.</p><ul><li>repeatable instruction-like accounting</li><li>natural per-invocation budget</li><li>higher execution overhead</li></ul></article>
        <article><div><span>epochs</span>{{ status("exploration", "Candidate") }}</div><h3>Lower-overhead time-like slices</h3><p>An independent ticker calls <code>Engine::increment_epoch</code>. <code>epoch_deadline_async_yield_and_update(delta)</code> yields and arms the next deadline.</p><ul><li>lighter compiled-code checks</li><li>good executor-latency control</li><li>timer scheduling makes slices nondeterministic</li></ul></article>
      </div>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>Neither mechanism fixes a blocking host function.</strong>
        <p>Fuel and epoch checks run while Wasm instructions run. If guest execution enters a synchronous Rust host function that blocks on a socket or mutex, the worker remains blocked. Authority-bearing I/O must use bounded async host functions—or an explicitly isolated blocking pool—not merely an async outer function.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="almanac-code-block pseudo-code-block">
        <div class="code-block-label"><span>Future shape · pseudocode, not current Dekopon API</span>{{ status("exploration", "Illustrative") }}</div>
        <pre><code><span class="code-purple">let mut</span> config = wasmtime::Config::new();
config
    .async_support(<span class="code-green">true</span>)
    .epoch_interruption(<span class="code-green">true</span>)
    .consume_fuel(<span class="code-green">true</span>);

<span class="code-comment">// One fresh Store per authorized invocation.</span>
<span class="code-purple">let mut</span> store = Store::new(&amp;engine, InvocationState::new(grant));
store.set_fuel(total_budget)?;
store.epoch_deadline_async_yield_and_update(<span class="code-orange">1</span>);

<span class="code-comment">// Narrow linker imports await Tokio I/O; no ambient client is exposed.</span>
linker.instance(<span class="code-green">"dekopon:provider-io"</span>)?
    .func_wrap_async(<span class="code-green">"request"</span>, bounded_request);

<span class="code-comment">// Total wall time still cancels the whole operation.</span>
<span class="code-purple">let</span> result = tokio::time::timeout(
    invocation_deadline,
    bindings.call_invoke_async(&amp;<span class="code-purple">mut</span> store, args),
).await??;</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p>The exact generated call name, WIT interface, ticker, and choice of fuel versus epochs are intentionally pseudocode. Current Dekopon has Wasmtime async support disabled and calls <code>call_invoke</code> synchronously.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="future-shape" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.4 · one broker process</span>
        <h2>Share expensive code, not mutable invocation state.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="tokio-process-board">
        <div class="tokio-process-title"><span>ONE <code>dekopon-brokerd</code> OS PROCESS</span><strong>bounded Tokio runtime*</strong><small>*worker count and executor split are open</small></div>
        <div class="tokio-shared-row">
          <div><span>shared, thread-safe</span><strong>Wasmtime Engine</strong><small>configuration + code registry</small></div>
          <div><span>shared, immutable</span><strong>compiled Component cache</strong><small>amortized compilation</small></div>
          <div><span>shared, bounded</span><strong>admission semaphore</strong><small>caps active stores / memory</small></div>
        </div>
        <div class="tokio-worker-row">
          <div class="worker-column"><span>worker thread 1</span><div class="tokio-task task-a"><strong>invocation A future</strong><small>poll guest → await host I/O</small></div><div class="tokio-task task-c"><strong>audit task</strong><small>runs while A is pending</small></div></div>
          <div class="worker-column"><span>worker thread 2</span><div class="tokio-task task-b"><strong>invocation B future</strong><small>guest yields at epoch/fuel slice</small></div><div class="tokio-task task-a"><strong>invocation A resumes</strong><small>a task may resume on another worker</small></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="tokio-store-row">
          <div><strong>Store A</strong><span>fresh memory · grant A · limits A</span></div>
          <div><strong>Store B</strong><span>fresh memory · grant B · limits B</span></div>
          <div><strong>Store C</strong><span>fresh memory · grant C · limits C</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>A Store is mutably borrowed while its call future is polled; it is not simultaneously driven by multiple workers. Tokio may move a <code>Send</code> task between worker threads at suspension points, but there is still one logical owner of that Store. Independent Stores provide concurrency.</p>

      <div class="capacity-equation"><span>maximum active stores</span><b>×</b><span>per-Store memory ceiling</span><b>+</b><span>fiber stacks + host buffers</span><b>≤</b><strong>broker memory budget</strong></div>
      <p>Fresh stores improve isolation but do not make concurrency free. The broker needs admission control over active invocations, aggregate memory, outbound requests, open bodies, and audit pressure—not only a per-Store <code>64 MiB</code> limit copied from immediate mode.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="lua" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.5 · alternative embedding</span>
        <h2>Why components instead of something like Lua?</h2>
        <p>Lua is an excellent embedded language. The meaningful comparison is not “fast versus slow”; it is which boundary Dekopon wants to standardize and defend.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table lua-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Dimension</th><th scope="col">Wasm component + Wasmtime</th><th scope="col">Embedded Lua</th><th scope="col">Dekopon consequence</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Guest languages</th><td>Any toolchain that can target compatible core Wasm and component bindings.</td><td>Lua source/bytecode, plus native host modules where allowed.</td><td>Wasm supports a provider ecosystem without choosing one source language.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Interface contract</th><td>WIT is typed, inspectable, language-neutral, and versionable; canonical ABI is standardized.</td><td>Dynamic values through stack API, tables, functions, and userdata; conventions are embedder-defined.</td><td>WIT makes reviewable imports/exports and generated bindings first-class.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Authority surface</th><td>Only linked imports are callable; current Dekopon’s import set is empty.</td><td>Only libraries/functions placed into the Lua state are callable; dangerous standard libraries can be omitted.</td><td>Both can be least-privilege. Wasm packages the requested import contract with the artifact.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Memory isolation</th><td>Guest linear memory is bounds-checked and separate from the host heap; fresh Store per call.</td><td>Separate managed Lua heap, but userdata and native C modules can bridge directly into host objects.</td><td>Wasm’s pointer model better matches hostile third-party components; neither protects a compromised embedder.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Startup / footprint</th><td>Validation and native compilation are heavier; shared engine and compiled cache amortize them.</td><td>Small runtime and fast load are longstanding strengths.</td><td>Lua can win for tiny trusted scripts and rapid local policy-like customization.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Boundary overhead</th><td>Canonical lifting/lowering; current Dekopon adds inner JSON serialization.</td><td>Direct stack/table conversions are often cheap, though dynamic checks and allocations remain.</td><td>Dekopon should remove avoidable JSON before blaming the component model.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Cooperative work</th><td>Async host calls suspend Wasmtime fibers; fuel or epoch instrumentation can yield CPU-heavy guest code.</td><td>Coroutines yield explicitly; debug hooks/instruction counters can interrupt or arrange cooperative slices, with limits around host/native calls.</td><td>Both require the embedder to bound blocking host code and define fairness.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Distribution</th><td>Portable component artifact plus WIT package; strong content-addressing/signing story is available.</td><td>Readable source or bytecode, simple deployment, but bytecode portability/version rules need care.</td><td>Wasm aligns with multi-language signed provider artifacts and registry tooling.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Debuggability</th><td>Native compilation, generated adapters, and ABI layers complicate traces; component tooling helps.</td><td>Source-level scripting and interactive inspection are usually simpler.</td><td>Lua remains attractive for trusted operator scripting, even if providers stay components.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="choose-runtime-grid">
        <article><span>Choose Lua when…</span><h3>The code is small, trusted, and operator-authored.</h3><p>You value instant iteration, a tiny embedder, dynamic host objects, and source-level debugging more than a language-neutral binary contract.</p></article>
        <article><span>Choose Wasm components when…</span><h3>The artifact is third-party, polyglot, and capability-reviewed.</h3><p>You want typed import/export metadata, separate linear memory, portable toolchains, explicit linking, and runtime resource controls.</p></article>
        <article><span>Use both when…</span><h3>Customization and integration are different products.</h3><p>Trusted local orchestration rules could use a script language while untrusted provider plugins remain Wasm components. This is an option, not a Dekopon commitment.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="decision" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.6 · design decision frame</span>
        <h2>What is fixed, and what must be measured.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="decision-ledger">
        <article><div>{{ status("current", "Current") }}</div><h3>Synchronous immediate mode</h3><ul><li>one shared runtime mutex</li><li>blocking model transport</li><li>fresh Store and instance per call</li><li>fuel cap + epoch deadline trap</li><li>no Wasmtime async Cargo feature</li></ul></article>
        <article><div>{{ status("direction", "Committed direction") }}</div><h3>Broker-owned async host</h3><ul><li>shared engine and compiled cache</li><li>fresh bounded Store per invocation</li><li>narrow asynchronous host functions</li><li>Tokio integration</li><li>separate broker process for effect authority</li></ul></article>
        <article><div>{{ status("exploration", "Must be proven") }}</div><h3>Scheduler policy</h3><ul><li>fuel versus epoch slices—or both</li><li>quantum and total budgets</li><li>worker versus dedicated executor pool</li><li>admission and aggregate memory limits</li><li>cancellation, cleanup, and audit ordering</li></ul></article>
      </div>

